WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:15.789
Hey Wisecrack, not-a-pickle Jared here, and
today we’re breaking down the season finale

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:17.849
of Rick and Morty Season 3.

00:00:17.849 --> 00:00:22.800
In this season especially, Rick and Morty continues to flaunt the meaninglessness of our existence.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:23.720
Nobody gets it.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.400
Nothing you think matters matters.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.300
This isn't special. This - this is happening infinite times across infinite realities.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.460
Every time the show’s teased us with a authentic
moment of love, or emotion,

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:35.700
it’s ripped it right out from under us.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.460
My grandpa was my hero.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.160
Your mom wouldn't have accepted me if I came home without you and your sister,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.800
so now you know the real reason I rescued you. I just took over the family, Morty!

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.350
This leaves an interesting challenge for creating
a tv show: how do you have characters grow

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.000
when there is no meaning?

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.500
Well, the show gives an novel solution, that’s
a little better than just spiraling deeper into nihilism.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:00.400
Guarantee you're gonna die if you touch me, and there's no afterlife everything just goes black. Don't do it.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.800
And that growth isn’t from the titular protagonist,
but the Matriarch of the Smith family.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.660
Welcome to this Wisecrack Quicktake on The
Rickchurian Mortydate.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.120
And, of course, spoilers ahead.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.600
Ok, so first a quick recap: Rick and Morty
are called in to help the president with some

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.050
X-Files shenanigans, but soon bore of helping
out the leader of the free world.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.600
Rick and the president begin an ever-escalating
dick-waving contest, the two unleash their

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.060
vast arsenals against each other, before Rick
has a more pressing matter to attend to: Jerry.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:30.400
Oh! Damnit.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.340
The more interesting subplot, however, is Beth’s.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:38.180
Fresh off the events of Episode 9, Beth begins
to question whether or not she had taken Rick’s

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.320
offer to be replaced by a clone, 
and if she, in fact, is the clone.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.170
To settle the matter, she seeks the help of
Jerry who recreates one of their first dates

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:50.930
and rekindles their love. But still worried
she’s gone Blade Runner, Beth runs off to

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:56.000
the woods with the family, where Rick shows
up to kill Jerry and convince her that she’s not a clone.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.740
In our last quicktake, we talked about Rick
informing Beth of what her intelligence actually means.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:01.920
"Am I evil?

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.600
Worse. You’re smart.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.100
When you know nothing matters the universe is yours and I’ve never met a universe that was into it."

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.510
Rick’s answer to all of this, as we know
know, is “Don’t think about it.”

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.500
Faced with this, Beth is given radical freedom
to choose her destiny.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:20.280
And this freedom brings her solace about her
choice to stay with, or leave, her family

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.520
- regardless of which choice she makes.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.080
"I don't know if I can do it.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:27.920
Then stay and luxuriate in a life you can finally know you've chosen.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.980
My secret bonus is that no matter what you choose you're finally gonna chill the f*** out."

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.460
But this episode SEEMS to 
suggest that she chose to stay,

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.800
"Beth, you crazy bitch, you're my daughter."

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.520
and has a newfound sense of happiness.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.220
She’s being a better mother to Summer, sort of,

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.680
"I just put it on for fun. I know I can't buy it.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.500
Oh come on, you look great! You're only young once.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.480
Just promise if the results are too 
strong you'll use protection."

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.380
finally loves Jerry, and has brought her 
family back together.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.400
"We're a real family now. In many 
ways things will be like season 1."

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:02.560
But for some, it may seem kind of...weird
that she just suddenly rekindles her love

00:03:02.569 --> 00:03:05.170
with the helpless parasite that is Jerry.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.990
We could apply some of the philosophical themes
we’ve identified in earlier quick takes

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:13.410
here: Sartre’s bad faith and radical freedom,
alienation vs agency.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:17.660
But I’m going to propose something a little
new to explain why Beth reconnects with Jerry

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.660
and her overall paradigm shift.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.760
Amor Fati - the cure for common nihilism.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.880
Now, what really made me think of this is this moment:

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.940
Beth, worried she’s a clone, must now confront
and question her past.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.930
Not only is she still thinking about Rick’s
original proposition - the ability to flee

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.720
her past - but as a possible clone, are
those memories even her own, and what does

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.130
that mean for her future, or her happiness?

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:58.500
Amor Fati is a latin phrase Freddy Nietzschmeister
used that means “Love of Fate.”

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.450
Amor Fati is about the affirmation of life,
being what Nietzsche called a “Yes-Sayer,”

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.700
not to be confused with the pervasive corporate
“Yes-Man”, and it’s tied to a thought

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.410
experiment many refer to as “Eternal Recurrence.”

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:15.060
To simplify, imagine a demon shows up and
says he’s going to groundhog day your whole life.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:20.100
Do you get thrown into an existential despair
because you have to infinitely relive questionable

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:25.200
life moments like the popularity of Smash Mouth
and frosting your tips in middle school, or

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.080
do you, to paraphrase Nietzsche, high-five
the demon

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.700
and say you’ve never heard anything more divine.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.060
To want to relive everything that’s happened
in your life for eternity - the good, the

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:40.460
bad, and everything in between, is the ultimate
affirmation of life, it’s amor fati.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.870
And it’s part of Nietzsche’s grand plan
to escape the crushing nihilism of existence.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:50.440
Given Beth’s existential crisis and proclivity
for Rick-like disillusionment-causing intelligence,

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.120
Beth has to confront her past.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:56.120
"I came to you for help and now I'm insulting your intelligence and look where intelligence gets you!"

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:58.920
And, with Jerry, she gets to re-live a small
part of it..

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:01.260
"I just thoughtt f*** it! Lips don't sweat!"

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:07.060
And while she may have hated her life the
first time around, including, her date with

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.990
Jerry, this time around she’ll be that 
Nietzschean Yes-Sayer.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:13.669
But I don’t think Beth is rewriting the past, either.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:16.550
She doesn’t deny the years of misery she has endured.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:22.510
Amor Fati isn’t about some kind of “everything
is a blessing in disguise,” but it's more about not to shun

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:24.130
the ugly aspects of our lives.

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:29.500
As she tells Jerry, she’s no longer the
woman he married, And sure, Jerry may be an idiot,

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.300
*whisper* loooosssserrr

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.640
but maybe “simplicity” is what
a person with a family history of using intelligence

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.830
to justify sickness needs.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:42.730
We see another amor fati-esque moment when
Beth thinks Rick has come to retire her:

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.500
Brushing aside the Blade Runner-yness of it
all, Beth is also functionally saying: Yes, a

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:01.020
version of Beth may have chosen to leave,
but I embrace being left with my family rather

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:02.500
than dwell on what could have been.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.300
And, if you’ll let me extrapolate a bit,
while there might be infinite universes where

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:11.520
Rick and Beth have made infinitely different
choices, she wants to stay in this one.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.720
As Nietzsche says

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.860
While Nietzsche didn’t necessarily philosophize
about infinite realities, he did write about

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.280
the impossible task of “knowing oneself.”

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:32.040
"I'm out of excuses to not be who I am. So, who am I?

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.120
What do I do?"

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.740
Beth doesn’t know who she is, but desperately wants to.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:40.480
For Nietzsche, the logic of why we actually
do anything is impenetrable.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:44.880
And, if we want to apply this Nietzschean
framing to Rick, we the fans almost never

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.700
know what actually drives him.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:48.000
"Why would you do that for me?

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.520
I don't know. Maybe you matter so little that I like you. Or maybe, it makes you matter. Maybe I love you."

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.620
So while Beth can fret about whether or not
she chose to be cloned, or whether or not

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:01.180
any of it matters in the infinite versions
of herself, Nietzsche would say it really doesn’t

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.680
matter - all we can do is love our fate.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.669
And that seems to be the choice Beth has made.

00:07:06.669 --> 00:07:10.960
And to me, this is really interesting, because
while the show has often portrayed Rick as

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.980
a kind of Nietzschean ubermensch at war with
the meaningless cosmos.

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:17.020
There is no God! In your face!

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:20.120
But Rick, in a quite un-Ubermenschy way, 
doesn’t really create

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:22.600
his own new meaning as much as he just kind

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:25.340
of dicks around in the most self destructive
way possible.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:30.620
Beth, on the other hand, when faced with the
meaninglessness of the cosmos, finds happiness.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:33.100
And that’s pretty ubermenschy, if you ask me.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.050
Now, if you’re like me and don’t know
what to do with yourself now that you have

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:41.520
to wait probably forever for season 4, consider
checking our Wisecrack podcast, The Squanch.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:44.320
Where we broke down every episode of season 3.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.900
Over the break, we’ll be breaking down older
episodes, engaging a lot more with your questions,

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.640
and trying desperately to interview anyone
who works on the show.

